  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nForecast hasn't changed significantly with regards to the next \nsystem. The low pressure system will stall off the coast of \ncentral California today and stay there into Wednesday. The low \nwill begin to move inland on Thursday and then weaken and pass \nover eastern Idaho Friday. The low will depart the region by \nSaturday. Expect isolated showers and storms Today through \nWednesday with very little rainfall expected. Rain chances \nincrease on Thursday with rain likely overspreading the region \nThursday night into Friday. The rain should come to an end by \nSaturday morning. Rainfall amounts are still quite uncertain and\ndependent upon rain intensity and the exact track of the low \nthrough the region. The mean qpf from the NBM continues to show \n1.00 to 1.50 inches across East Idaho with 0.50 to 1.00 over the\nMagic Valley into the central mountains. The 10th percentile \nstill supports 0.50 to 1.00 inches for East Idaho with 0.10 to \n0.50 inches for the Magic Valley and central mountains. However \nthe 90th percentile paints 1.50 to 2.00 inches across the entire\nregion. The forecast qpf from WPC favors the 25th percentile. \nSnow with this event will probably not be terribly impactful. \nSnow levels may fall to pass level as the precipitation is about\nto exit the region. Temperatures will continue to run above \nnormal into Thursday. Looks like a 20 to 25 degree drop in \ntemperatures between Thursday and Friday for highs.",
